      "title": "Ou khatta",
      "odia": "ଓଉ ଖଟା",
      "lead": "A little sweet, a little sour. The bowl at the edge of the meal has a story of its own.",
      "hero": "ou-khatta",
      "save_id": "food:ou-khatta",
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "taste",
          "title": "The small bowl that changes the meal",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Give the accompaniment a moment of its own. Notice the fruit, the sauce, the seeds; then ask the cook how this version found its way to the table.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            },
            {
              "text": "Ou khatta is an elephant-apple relish. In Sasmita’s sweet version, boiled pieces meet jaggery or sugar, ginger, a seed tempering and coconut.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-ou-sasmita2017"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "origin",
          "title": "A meal remembered through one dish",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Julie Acharya Ray remembers ou khatta among the dishes in her mother’s Kartik meals. Her 2014 account gives this bowl a place in one family’s calendar.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-ou-ray2014"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "Food memories can be wonderfully specific: a particular kitchen, a particular person, a taste you keep looking for. Which dish brings you home?",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "making",
          "title": "Two versions, one cook’s curiosity",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Sasmita’s later sweet-and-sour version introduces mustard–cumin paste with jaggery and panch phoron. It leaves out onion and garlic. Read the two preparations side by side to see how one cook varies a familiar dish.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-ou-sasmita2019"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "The two recipes come from the same author. They invite comparison, without defining how every household should cook ou khatta.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-ou-sasmita2017",
                "food-ou-sasmita2019"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "try",
          "title": "Bring curiosity to the table",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Ask whether the serving is sweet, sour or both, and what goes into its tempering. Mention ingredients you avoid, including mustard, before ordering.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            },
            {
              "text": "Save it as a dish to seek out, then confirm availability with your host or cook. We have not reviewed a kitchen. If someone shares a family memory, ask before recording or publishing it.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "related": [
        {
          "title": "The everyday Odisha table",
          "href": "/food/everyday/"
        },
        {
          "title": "Badi chura: another small bowl",
          "href": "/food/badi-chura/"
        },
        {
          "title": "Meet an Odia meal in Bhubaneswar",
          "href": "/food/bhubaneswar/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "area": "Across Odisha",
      "planning_area": "Across Odisha",
      "regions": [
        "Across Odisha"
      ],
      "checked_on": "2026-10-01",
      "slug": "badi-chura",
      "title": "Badi chura",
      "odia": "ବଡ଼ି ଚୁରା",
      "lead": "A handful of crumbs, a generous amount of character. Meet the little accompaniment that deserves a closer look.",
      "hero": "badi-chura",
      "save_id": "food:badi-chura",
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "taste",
          "title": "Look closer at the crumbs",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Not every food story arrives as the centrepiece. Here, the texture draws you in: coarse pieces, flecks of onion, a bowl small enough to overlook.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            },
            {
              "text": "Badi chura begins with dried black-gram dumplings. Sasmita’s preparation fries or dry-roasts them before crushing them with onion, garlic, chilli and mustard oil.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-badi-sasmita2017"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "origin",
          "title": "Before the crushing, the drying",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Two stages sit behind the name: making sun-dried badi, then turning it into chura. The documented preparation keeps the mixture coarse.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-badi-sasmita2017"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "Ask who made the badi and how they learned. A conversation about one small ingredient can lead to another person, another season, another kitchen.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "meal",
          "title": "A companion for pakhala",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Pritisha Das includes badi chura with pakhala in her account for Odisha Tourism. It is one named picture of an Odia meal, with room for other households to tell theirs.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-soul-pritisha2024"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "Follow that connection into the rest of the meal. Ask what your host likes alongside pakhala, and why. Let the answer shape what you try next.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "try",
          "title": "Ask about this serving",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "The documented version contains onion, garlic, chilli and mustard oil. Ask the maker about the actual ingredients and how recently it was prepared; do not assume every bowl follows the same method.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-badi-sasmita2017"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "Save badi chura as a tasting idea. No shop, shelf life or local availability is verified here. A maker’s name and a place would make a useful contribution to this growing story.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "related": [
        {
          "title": "Odisha cooks with fire",
          "href": "/food/fire-cooking/"
        },
        {
          "title": "The everyday Odisha table",
          "href": "/food/everyday/"
        },
        {
          "title": "Pakhala and the Bhubaneswar table",
          "href": "/food/bhubaneswar/#pakhala"
        },
        {
          "title": "Make room for Ou khatta",
          "href": "/food/ou-khatta/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "area": "Across Odisha",
      "planning_area": "Across Odisha",
      "regions": [
        "Across Odisha"
      ],
      "checked_on": "2026-10-01",
      "slug": "tala-pitha",
      "title": "Tala pitha",
      "odia": "ତାଳ ପିଠା",
      "lead": "A golden slice and a browned edge. Follow ripe palm fruit into one of Odisha’s seasonal food stories.",
      "hero": "tala-pitha",
      "save_id": "food:tala-pitha",
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "taste",
          "title": "Begin with the cut slice",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Look at the colour inside the crust. The photograph shows tala poda pitha: one answer to what a cook can make with ripe palm fruit.",
              "source_ids": [
                "photo-tala-pitha"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "The Gapu Photography account credits Manaswini Sahoo of Soul Of Odisha with a tala poda pitha preparation. Ripe palm fruit is its starting point.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-tala-manaswini2018"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "origin",
          "title": "A taste with a season attached",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "That account places the fruit in a monsoon setting. It gives us a seasonal association to explore, rather than an exact availability calendar for every part of Odisha.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-tala-manaswini2018"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "Ask a local cook when they look for the fruit and how they recognise it at the stage they want. Plan around their answer; a seasonal dish may be worth returning for.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "making",
          "title": "One fruit, more than one food",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "The featured account names tala poda pitha. Keep that specific preparation in view when exploring the wider name tala pitha; the page does not establish one method for every version.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-tala-manaswini2018"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "Ripe-fruit preparations and palm-seed foods are separate stories. We are still documenting the precise seed stage and local usage of the name tala khaja; it should not be treated as another name for this cake.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "try",
          "title": "Let the maker introduce it",
          "paragraphs": [
