      "title": "Enduri pitha",
      "odia": "ଏଣ୍ଡୁରି ପିଠା",
      "lead": "Enduri brings the leaf, the filling and the occasion into the same parcel. Looking at how it is prepared helps explain why the leaf matters.",
      "area": "Across Odisha",
      "planning_area": "Across Odisha",
      "regions": [
        "Across Odisha"
      ],
      "hero": "enduri-pitha",
      "save_id": "food:enduri-pitha",
      "checked_on": "2026-10-21",
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "taste",
          "title": "Begin with the leaf",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Turmeric leaf gives Enduri more than its wrapper. Odisha Tourism singles out its aroma alongside the coconut-and-jaggery filling. The leaf is part of what the dish offers before the first bite.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-tourism"
              ],
              "locator": "The Taste of Odisha, Enduri Pitha heading, line304"
            },
            {
              "text": "When a parcel is opened, take a moment to look at the leaf and the pitha separately. Then notice the filling. A photograph can show their arrangement; it cannot tell you everything that went into this particular preparation.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "original reader invitation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "origin",
          "title": "A parcel in a family celebration",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "In her January 2018 account of winter observances, Namita Sarangi places Enduri within Prathamastami’s attention to the eldest child. She describes bathing and dressing the child in new clothes from the maternal home, then offering turmeric-leaf Enduri with other foods to Sasthi before bandapana. The parcel belongs to an occasion involving clothing, food and blessings, not just a seasonal menu.",
              "source_ids": [
                "osha-kanji-sarangi2018"
              ],
              "locator": "Original Odia paragraph beginning Margasira Krishna Ashtami, web172, before Kanji Anla transition"
            },
            {
              "text": "Sarangi also names Enduri among the preparations for Manabasa, the Lakshmi observance described in the next paragraph. Its strong Prathamastami association is therefore not an only-on-that-day rule. Her essay records a particular cultural description; it does not establish when Enduri began or how every household makes it.",
              "source_ids": [
                "osha-kanji-sarangi2018"
              ],
              "locator": "Manabasa paragraph web173, offering list contains ଏଣ୍ଡୁରି"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "making",
          "title": "What happens inside the parcel",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Lipi Das’s 2015 ICAR-CRRI compilation documents a rice–black-gram batter that is left to ferment. It is spread on turmeric leaves, with filling in the centre, before the leaves are folded. This version combines grated coconut and paneer with jaggery or sugar, cardamom and pepper. The dairy ingredient is easy to miss if you know the dish only through its appearance.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-crri-rice2015"
              ],
              "locator": "Printed19/PDF30 ingredients and Method firsttwo paragraphs"
            },
            {
              "text": "The book gives two steaming arrangements: a steamer, or a wide pot with muslin tied across its mouth. The parcels sit on the cloth beneath a lid. The second arrangement makes the work of the vessel clear: water below, supported parcels above, steam enclosed around them.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-crri-rice2015"
              ],
              "locator": "Printed19/PDF30 Method lastparagraph; explanatory restatement not testedengineering advice"
            },
            {
              "text": "Das’s preface credits women of Ananya Mahila Bikas Samiti in Sankilo, Cuttack, among the contributors to the compilation. The book combines primary and secondary information; it does not identify this particular Enduri preparation with one named household.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-crri-rice2015"
              ],
              "locator": "PDF6 Preface; avoid attributing Enduri specifically togroup/village"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "try",
          "title": "Ask about this version",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Ask the maker what is inside this version, and who taught them to prepare it. Let their answer carry its own name and place.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "locator": "Printed19 filling supports initial question; remainder original enquiry invitation",
              "kind": "original enquiry invitation, grounded by preceding sourced ingredient account"
            },
            {
              "text": "If someone is preparing Enduri for an observance, ask how they would like the occasion and food to be described. Make any visit or purchase arrangement directly. A festival association is a reason to enquire, not a promise that a parcel will be waiting.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "original practical invitation; no availability or household access implied"
            }
          ]
        }
