      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "taste",
          "title": "More than one way to make poda",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "The photograph shows one serving. Written accounts help us look beyond its appearance.",
              "source_ids": [],
              "kind": "editorial_invitation"
            },
            {
              "text": "Lipi Das’s 2015 ICAR-CRRI compilation records khira poda: rice batter and milk cooked into dough, then cooked further in a greased pan.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-crri-rice2015"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "making",
          "title": "A mixture changes the method",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Its jaggery poda uses rice flour stirred into sweetened liquid, banana-leaf lining and covered cooking. Another arrangement turns the preparation to cook both sides. Biri chaula poda combines separately ground rice and black gram with sweet ingredients; the batter rests before cooking in a cooker.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-crri-rice2015"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "The compilation identifies neither each recipe’s household nor one original recipe.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-crri-rice2015"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "variations",
          "title": "Two accounts, different leaves",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "In Priyadarshini Chatterjee’s 2023 report, writer Sujata Dehury describes a coastal rice-flour-and-jaggery mixture wrapped in banana leaf or cooked in a leaf-lined vessel. Food researcher Sweta Biswal describes a western version: lightly seasoned rice-and-black-gram batter baked in stitched sal-leaf parcels.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-poda-regions-mint2023"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "Biswal’s savoury preparation accompanies curry, unlike the sweetened rice-and-black-gram version in Das’s compilation. These accounts do not establish rules for every regional kitchen.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-poda-regions-mint2023",
                "food-crri-rice2015"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "season",
          "title": "Preparing before the celebration",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "text": "Dehury connects making food ahead of Raja with the festival’s pause in women’s kitchen work. Biswal also places poda pitha around Pusha Puni in parts of western Odisha. These named accounts give the dish more than one seasonal setting; they do not establish a single festival of origin.",
              "source_ids": [
                "food-poda-regions-mint2023"
              ],
              "kind": "sourced_summary"
            },
            {
              "text": "These accounts leave the earliest history open: who first made poda pitha, and where its name began.",
